winex11: Do not draw a window surface over client surface windows.

A window's X children are its client surface windows: the X windows a
Vulkan or OpenGL client surface presents to when it is onscreen. The window
surface draws with IncludeInferiors, so whatever of it is flushed lands on
top of them. Its region normally keeps it off the client area -- the server
excludes the client rectangle of a window with a pixel format -- but that
region is only updated when the window's state is, on the window's thread.
A client surface created on another thread, as a renderer thread does,
leaves a stretch where the surface still covers the client area, and any
flush in that stretch (an expose, a shape change) paints its contents over
the client surface's frame. A surface no one painted is white since
18339eb0, so the window flashes white until the client surface presents
again.

Draw with ClipByChildren instead. The client area of a window with a pixel
format is excluded from the surface anyway, so this only changes what the
races above show.

In Affinity under WPF hardware rendering, the main window turned white for
572-725 ms at every start, between the splash and the interface, measured
from what KWin composites at 120 captures a second. With this, 0 ms, in
three starts.

diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c
index 1f91040..1b9c888 100644
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c
@@ -1945,7 +1945,11 @@ static struct window_surface *create_surface( HWND hwnd, Window window, const XV
         surface->byteswap = byteswap;
         surface->window = window;
         surface->gc = XCreateGC( gdi_display, window, 0, NULL );
-        XSetSubwindowMode( gdi_display, surface->gc, IncludeInferiors );
+        /* The window's only X children are client surface windows, which the
+         * surface must not draw over: they are clipped out of its region once
+         * the window state is updated, but that can come late, when another
+         * thread created the client surface. */
+        XSetSubwindowMode( gdi_display, surface->gc, ClipByChildren );
     }
 
     return window_surface;
